# Open Code and composition

shadcn’s core product idea applies here: this is **how you build** a TfL component library (source in the consumer repo), not a sealed npm UI package. Pair with frozen layering in [docs/product-architecture.md](docs/product-architecture.md) and decisions J3/C6 in [docs/ia-migration/DECISIONS.md](docs/ia-migration/DECISIONS.md).

## Open Code

- Ship **component source** via the shadcn registry + CLI. Consumers own and edit files after install.
- Document install with the real registry URL pattern used in this project:

```bash
pnpm dlx shadcn@latest add https://tfl-components.vercel.app/r/<name>.json
```

- Prefer extending owned source (or composing wrappers in the **app**) over fighting a black-box dependency.
- Do **not** treat `components/ui/` lightly as a place for drive-by edits; extend via composition when possible. Domain components live under `components/tfl/` (and related), not as forks of every shadcn atom.

## Distribution

- The **registry schema + CLI path** is part of the product, not an afterthought to the docs site.
- Every installable Interface / Primitive docs page should expose a working install command when the item is published to `/r`.
- Keep registry payloads accurate: files, dependencies, and CSS/token assumptions the consumer needs.
- Blocks are compositions: document which registry items to install, unless we explicitly publish a block package.

## Composition contract (layers)

```text
normalised tfl-ts data
        ↓
data-aware Interface (props)
        ↓
domain interpretation (sorting, severity, labels)
        ↓
rendering Primitives (explicit values)
        ↓
Foundations (colour, type, roundel rules)
        ↓
finished UI
```

Rules:

1. **Interfaces** accept (or document how to obtain) **normalised** data and render useful transport UI.  
2. **Primitives** take **explicit, already-resolved** values; they must remain useful without `tfl-ts` where practical.  
3. **Foundations** are shared brand/visual language and licensing — not a second component tree.  
4. Do **not** fork “static vs live” product lines for the same concept.  
5. Do **not** duplicate Tube-status / Bus-status trees; domains are variants or filters on one intent.

## Data as props (hard rule)

Reusable library boards and data-aware components take **normalised data as props**. Fetching belongs in the **app**, **docs demo**, or **Block** — not inside the published component API.

```tsx
// ✅ GOOD — board is presentational over normalised rows
export function TubeStatusBoard({ data }: { data: NormalisedLineStatus[] }) {
  return /* … */;
}

// App / docs / Block:
const data = await getStatuses(); // or tfl-ts client
return <TubeStatusBoard data={data} />;
```

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — fetch-inside as the library API
export function TubeStatusBoard() {
  const data = useSWR("/api/status"); // hides contract; couples to site
  return /* … */;
}
```

Site helpers (e.g. `getCachedLineStatuses`) are fine for **this Next app** and demos. They are not the public component contract. Docs must show the props path first.

## Shared, predictable APIs

- Prefer one clear props shape per Interface; document variations (domains, layouts) as props or slots, not parallel components.  
- Primitives should expose visual control (scale, orientation, colours as values) without hidden domain fetches.  
- Diagram scale: one inherited `--tfl-diagram-scale` (see design-system docs); do not desync ticks/rings/labels with ad-hoc text utilities.  
- Use `cn()` for class merging; semantic tokens for chrome (`bg-background`, `text-muted-foreground`, `border-border`).

## AI / agent readiness

Agents (and humans) succeed when:

- Install command, data shape, and render snippet are adjacent and short  
- Props and normalised types are named consistently with `tfl-ts`  
- Page anatomy order matches frozen docs  
- Conflicts with frozen IA are written to `docs/ia-migration/CONFLICTS.md` (or equivalent), not silently patched  

When adding or changing a component, update: source, registry entry (if installable), demo loader, MDX, and catalog entry together when those surfaces exist.

## Anti-patterns

- Publishing a component that only works with site-local fetch caches  
- “Convenient” `fetch` defaults inside `components/tfl/**` that become the documented API  
- New APIs that ignore existing Interface/Primitive splits  
- Registry entries that omit required files or peer dependencies  
- Wrapping every TfL visual in an opaque npm package instead of Open Code  
